{"_data": [{"contents": [{"content": "", "type": "lesson_name"}, {"content": "The 80's", "type": "unit_name"}, {"content": 4, "type": "position"}, {"content": "stub", "type": "origin"}, {"content": 1, "type": "lesson"}, {"content": "A2.2", "type": "level"}], "type": "metadata"}, {"contents": [{"content": "false", "type": "test"}], "type": "achievment_test"}, {"contents": [{"content": "", "type": "text"}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Describe common activities at work to a visitor using short sentences in familiar spoken and written situations.  Give detailed and important information regarding to the history of language programming in different periods of time.", "type": "primary"}, {"content": "Identifies the expressions and vocabulary necessary for the description of places of work and jobs in common written and spoken situations.  Describes common activities related to Software Programming through the use of appropriate expressions, grammar and vocabulary in written or spoken situations according to the abilities related to this level.", "type": "secondary_1"}], "type": "objectives"}, {"content": "enpe7n4le01ob04re1img01.jpg", "type": "background_image"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Simple Past Tense: Irregular Verbs", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "You use the <strong>simple past tense</strong> to talk about actions that started and finished in the past.", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "To form the <strong>simpe past tense</strong> you need to use the verbs in simple past. In this case, we are going to form the past simple with <strong>irregular verbs</strong>. First, let's take a look at the structure:</br></br>  <strong><p align=\"center\">Subject + Verb in past +  Complement</strong></p>  Example:</br></br> <i>I <strong>studied</strong> software programming last year.</i></br><i>She <strong>knew</strong> a lot about fifth-generation programming languages.</i></br></br>  In English, verbs are divided into regular and irregular. To form <strong>regular verbs</strong>, you just need to add \u2013<i>ed</i>, \u2013<i>d</i> or \u2013<i>ied</i> at  the end of the verb. On the other hand, <strong>irregular verbs</strong> have a particular conjugation. Let's see some examples: ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "{\"rows\":4,\"columns\":1,\"cells\":[\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<div align=\\\"center\\\">Irregular Verbs\\\"}\"],\"styles\":\" instruction_table_background\"}", "type": "table"}, {"content": "{\"rows\":9,\"columns\":2,\"cells\":[\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Infinitive\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Past Simple\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"begin\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"began\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"do\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"did\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"find\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"found\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"go\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"went\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"have\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"had\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"know\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"knew\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"teach\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"taught\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"be\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"was/were\\\"}\"],\"styles\":\" instruction_table_background intro_table_border\"}", "type": "table"}, {"content": "I found a lot of information but now I don't know how to organize it.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "I made a similar presentation last semester. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": " On the other hand, the Turing Machine was one of the first machines to store data.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "I had an excellent professor, his name is Mr. Morris. ", "type": "example"}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Questions With Prepositions: With and For", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "When talking in an informal way, you can use <strong>prepositions</strong> in questions. ", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "When a <strong>preposition</strong> becomes the <i>object</i> of a question, these ones are placed at the end of the interrogative sentence. Let's take a look at the structure:</br></br>  <strong><p align=\"center\">Wh-Question + Auxiliary (If applies) + Verb + Subject + Complement +?</p></strong></br>  Examples: </br></br><i> What is an imperative language?</i></br> <i>Who works with you?</i> </br></br>  However, if you need to ask a question with a preposition, the appropriate structure you need to use is:</br></br>  <p align=\"center\"><strong>Wh-Question + Auxiliary (If applies) + Verb + Subject + Complement + Preposition +?</strong></p>  Example:</br></br>  <i>What is an imperative language <strong>for</strong>?</i></br><i>Who do you work <strong>with</strong>?</i> </br></br>  Notice that the <strong>preposition</strong> must go at the end of the interrogative statement and not at the beginning.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "That requires a lot of research, Who are you working with?", "type": "example"}, {"content": "Wow! Who did you learn with? ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "What was the Analytical Engine for?", "type": "example"}, {"content": "What is that for?", "type": "example"}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Analytical Engine ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "a computer model suggested by Charles Babbage in 1837", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: The first key feature was the Analytical Engine.", "type": "example"}, {"content":